Oh boy, this feels good!

After a couple of years of playing (and about six months playing
*properly* - that is, taking my time, thinking things through and generally
giving Nethack the respect it deserves), SUCCESS!

Galahad the Knight (human, male, lawful) ascended to demigod-hood on 13th
July 2004, at 1609 GMT.  Oh glorious moment!

This was the second time I'd had a character that made it past Medusa.  (The
first was Atalanta the elven ranger, who you may remember "unwisely ate the
body of Pestilence".  Won't catch me making *that* mistake again!)

What can I atttribute the success to?  Here are some possibilities:

1. I got Excalibur at the earliest possible opportunity.  Used it until I
got to Gehennom, and then switched to Grayswandir.

2. I was crowned quite early.  Common wisdom has it that this isn't a good
thing because of the long prayer time out - but for a Knight, getting the
intrinsics isn't as easy as for some classes.

3. A spot of nurse dancing almost doubled my HP before I went on the Quest.

4. I managed to survive without MR until the Quest, and without reflection
until I reached the Castle (Perseus' statue).  The first polymorph trap I
stepped on before having MR turned me into a kobold and I didn't lose my
armour.  By the time I stepped on the second, I had a blessed bag of holding
with a spare mithril coat inside.

5. The castle wand.  Speed boots, T-shirt and a Cloak of Protection helped
get my AC into the -30s (it was -37 when I ascended, IIRC).

6. There seemed to be an abundance of identify and enchant weapon scrolls,
and the two magic markers I found early on also helped (I was able to
blessed-genocide L, h and R quite early).

7. Powerful pets at times - including a Jabberwock, who was offed by an
energy vortex (I think) in the upper levels of Gehennom.

Notable points about the game:

1. Perhaps the most fervently neutral level I've ever seen: DL9 had *three*
altars to Brigit, one of which was in a temple.  One was converted to Lugh
and served as my base camp, and the priestess of Brigit took huge piles of
zorkmids off me in exchange for protection.

2. I was accompanied by a pet woodchuck (from a polytrap) all the way
through Gehennom, but he didn't follow me from the Plane of Air to the Plane
of Fire (or he was killed in Air, I guess).  Shame, I was looking forward to
ascending with him.

3. Never cast a spell - except for *attempts* to do so, to induce confusion
for reading scrolls of gold detection on the Planes.

4. Never identified a ring of free action.

5. Never identified a ring of slow digestion until my final descent from
base to the vibrating square.

6. Never killed a shopkeeper (me or my pets) - appropriate behaviour for a
Knight I guess.

7. Never rode a steed.

Playing a Knight was interesting.  I've never been fond of them, despite the
decent weapins and armour.  They get burdened easily in the early game and
their pets aren't great (starve too easily).  Also I tend to avoid lawful
characters, as I like to be able to kill the dwarves in the mines and steal
their armour and pick axes.  So it was a major surprise to be so successful
with this one.

The Quest seemed very easy.  The only other quest I've completed is the
Ranger's, and the maze-like construction there was very tiresome.  Barbarian
and Monk quests have been my undoing in the past.  This one was pretty much
a walk in the park (although, of course, I needed my ring of levitation).

I never used the "jump" ability and I never rode a steed.  Basically I
played him as a complete combat-wombat (I don't know how else to play,
really).  Although I did have something like 170 MP at the end of the game,
I never used them, and indeed only learned one spell (protection).

Earth, Air and Water were quite straightforward.  Air was very nasty.
Astral was a whole new experience for me; I chose to go to the right, for no
reason at all, and got lucky.  I don't think I could have fought my way back
past Pestilence and his chums if it hadn't been the correct altar.

